.TH AENIGMA-START 8 "2026-08-12" "aenigma 5.0.0" "Aenigma Administration Scripts"
.SH NAME
aenigma-start \- enable and start the Aenigma systemd service
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aenigma-start
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aenigma-start
enables the
.B aenigma
systemd service so that it starts automatically on boot, then
(re)starts it immediately. It takes no options or arguments.
.PP
The script performs the following actions:
.IP \(bu 2
Runs
.B systemctl enable aenigma
so the service is started automatically on future boots.
.IP \(bu 2
Runs
.B systemctl restart aenigma
to start the service now. If the service is already running, it is
stopped and started again rather than left untouched.
.PP
.B aenigma-start
must be run as root.
.SH OPTIONS
.B aenigma-start
takes no command-line options.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The service was enabled and (re)started successfully.
.TP
.B 1
The script was not run as root, or either
.B systemctl
invocation failed (for example, if the
.B aenigma
unit file is missing or the service fails to start).
.SH EXAMPLES
Enable the service for boot and start it now:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo aenigma-start
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
.B aenigma-start
uses
.B systemctl restart
rather than
.BR "systemctl start" .
Running this script against an already-running service therefore
causes a brief service interruption (the running process is
stopped, then started again) rather than a no-op; this is worth
being aware of if the script is invoked as part of an automated
provisioning flow that may run against an already-active service.
.PP
No verification is performed after either
.B systemctl
call beyond its own exit status; the script does not check, for
example via
.BR "systemctl is-active" ,
that the service actually reached a running state. A unit that
starts and then immediately crashes may not be reflected as a
failure of this script, depending on the unit's
.B Type=
and restart configuration.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR aenigma-stop (8),
.BR aenigma-config (8)
.SH AUTHOR
Romulus-Emanuel Ruja <romulus.ruja@aenigma.ro>
